      "title": "The Susan Collins Effect",
      "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/11/us/politics/in-the-senate-collins-often-defies-trump-but-rarely-stops-him.html",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T16:58:49.653Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/11/us/politics/in-the-senate-collins-often-defies-trump-but-rarely-stops-him.html",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "In the Senate, Collins Often Defies Trump but Rarely Stops Him",
      "pageDescription": "A New York Times analysis of Senator Susan Collins’s voting record shows that she frequently breaks with Republicans but more seldom changes an outcome.",
      "evidenceText": "In the Senate, Collins Often Defies Trump but Rarely Stops Him - The New York Times You have a preview view of this article while we are checking your access. When we have confirmed access, the full article content will load. In the Senate, Collins Often Defies Trump but Rarely Stops Him A New York Times analysis of Senator Susan Collins’s voting record shows that she frequently breaks with Republicans but more seldom changes an outcome. Credit... Haiyun Jiang/The New York Times Supported by SKIP ADVERTISEMENT Listen · 12:35 min Share full article By Michael Gold Catie Edmondson and Andrea Fuller Michael Gold and Catie Edmondson, who cover Congress, reported from Washington. Andrea Fuller, a data reporter, analyzed thousands of Senate votes. Oct. 11, 2026 It was approaching midnight on the Senate floor, and a vote that had been meant to last 15 minutes had dragged on for nearly three hours as Senator Susan Collins, Republican of Maine, weighed whether to kill one of President Trump’s top priorities. It was hardly the first time she had faced such a choice. Throughout Mr. Trump’s two terms, Ms. Collins had frequently criticized the president and voted against his initiatives more often than anyone else in her party. She cherished her reputation as one of the few remaining independent-minded Republicans in the Senate, which helped explain her enduring popularity in her purple home state. Yet she had more rarely blocked the president’s agenda outright. On that night last summer, Ms. Collins had the chance to do so. The senator, the chairwoman of the committee that controls federal spending, was deeply opposed to a bill Mr. Trump had demanded to cancel $9 billion in foreign aid and public broadcasting money that Congress had approved. She had prepared an amendment to shield the Corporation for Public Broadcasting from the cuts, a move that had bipartisan support. But if she succeeded, conservatives loyal to the president would all but certainly refuse to back the spending cuts. Given Republicans’ slim majority, their opposition could have doomed the bill, blocking Mr. Trump’s plan altogether. Image Despite voting against President Trump’s initiatives more than any other Republican, Senator Susan Collins of Maine has rarely blocked the president’s agenda outright in his second term. Credit... Haiyun Jiang/The New York Times After hours of wavering, Ms. Collins decided not to try to make the change. In what amounted to a largely symbolic move, she voted “no,” on the bill, registering her opposition to revoking the spending even though it was clear the measure had enough Republican votes to pass. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. If you are in Reader mode please exit and log into your Times account, or subscribe for all of The Times. Thank you for your patience while we verify access. Already a subscriber? Log in . Want all of The Times? Subscribe . Related Content Advertisement SKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
